{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content CLAUDE.md'","aggregated_output":"# CLAUDE.md — Working in this template repo\r\n\r\nThis repo is a **meta-project**: its product is the reusable kit in\r\n[`project-trajectory/`](project-trajectory/) that *other* repos copy in to run a\r\ngated, requirement-traced development process. So work here improves templates\r\nand scripts, not a downstream application.\r\n\r\n> Looking for the agent guide that ships **to** new projects? That's\r\n> [`project-trajectory/AGENTS.template.md`](project-trajectory/AGENTS.template.md)\r\n> (scaffolds to `AGENTS.md`, with thin `CLAUDE.md`/`GEMINI.md` stubs pointing at\r\n> it). Don't confuse the two: this file governs editing the kit; that one governs\r\n> using the kit.\r\n\r\n> **`OWNER_SCRATCHPAD.md` is owner-only** — never read, cite, or act on it. It\r\n> holds the human owner's free-form notes; the working surfaces are\r\n> `docs/status.md`, the registries, and `docs/log.md`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## What we're optimizing for\r\n\r\nThe canonical purpose statement is the `PROJECT-VISION:` tag opening the root\r\n[README.md](README.md) — the same one-home pattern the kit ships downstream.\r\nIn short: the kit exists to make downstream code **maintainable and\r\ntrustworthy** — readable for humans and agents alike, deeply tested, and\r\nadvanced only through explicit approval gates. Every change here should make\r\nthat easier to achieve in a real project — or get out of the way.\r\n\r\n## Principles for editing the kit\r\n\r\n- **Dogfood the philosophy.** The templates preach single-source-of-truth,\r\n  decompose-don't-paraphrase, and generated-not-hand-maintained. Hold the kit to\r\n  the same bar: don't restate a rule in five files — state it in\r\n  [`PROCESS.md`](project-trajectory/PROCESS.md) and link to it.\r\n- **Keep scripts stdlib-preferred and cross-platform; dependencies need a\r\n  ledger row.** The kit's own scripts run on a clean Python 3.11+ on Windows\r\n  and POSIX, and today import stdlib only. The rule is \"no *unargued*\r\n  dependencies,\" not \"no dependencies\": a genuinely better tool may enter via a\r\n  reviewed row in [`docs/dependencies.md`](docs/dependencies.md) — stating what\r\n  it replaces and why — enforced by `tests/test_dependency_ledger.py`. Shipped\r\n  checks stay stdlib-*preferred* (a dependency there forces every adopter to\r\n  install it — rare, ideally never). Tools a *downstream* project needs (ruff,\r\n  pytest) are theirs to install; the kit must not require them to run its own\r\n  checks.\r\n- **Stack-agnostic core, Python-first reference.** The process and ID scheme are\r\n  language-neutral; concrete harness commands are Python examples clearly marked\r\n  as \"swap for your stack.\"\r\n- **Templates must stay copy-ready.** A `*.template.*` file should produce\r\n  something sensible the moment it's copied and filled — example/placeholder rows\r\n  end in `-000` so `trace.py` ignores them. A token the kit *mandates* into an\r\n  adopter's cell must mean something in **their** repo: a marker naming one of\r\n  this repo's own rulings cites a record they can never read.\r\n- **Don't let this repo drift from the template it ships.** VALUES may diverge\r\n  between the kit's template and this repo's own instance (owner dials, filled\r\n  registry rows, enabled sets); STRUCTURE must not (schema headers, launcher\r\n  command contracts, declared-section shapes) — `tests/test_dogfood_sync.py`\r\n  enforces it.\r\n- **Self-test before claiming done.** The per-commit bar is one declared\r\n  command, `run.cmd smoke` (`run_menu.py smoke` from a shell; its steps are\r\n  [`docs/stack.ini`](docs/stack.ini) `[run]`). It runs the smoke tier once: a\r\n  red tier FAILS the bar; its CPU-seconds and wall time are only reported\r\n  (owner, 2026-10-20). `run.cmd audit` judges them, and the tier's test\r\n  count, against `[smoke-budget]`, by hand and at phase close; a breach is\r\n  filed as a re-tier row, never charged to the lane that ran it. Heavy cases\r\n  live in `tests/conftest.py` `SLOW_MODULES`, every script family keeping an\r\n  in-process pin. A mid-phase WI/slice ends at that commit bar. Run the **full**\r\n  unfiltered suite (`python -m pytest -q -n auto`, ~10 min on a quiet box,\r\n  3–4× that while other sessions load it) once at phase close; the lane refresh\r\n  runs the full bar declared for its current stage in the merge slot. The\r\n  unfiltered suite bootstraps a temp scaffold and exercises every script\r\n  end-to-end. Paste the real output; never report a green you didn't produce.\r\n  Commit hooks are on before any work (`core.hooksPath` at `.githooks`, which\r\n  dev-setup sets); every agent route refuses without them, and no commit\r\n  bypasses them.\r\n  (Commit bar vs gate bar, and what the smoke tier drops: the\r\n  `session-protocol` skill §3.)\r\n- **Edit conservatively.** This is a foundation many projects inherit; prefer the\r\n  smallest change that fixes the problem, and flag anything that would force\r\n  downstream repos to migrate.\r\n- **Consolidate across the codebase.** The scope above is per-task; when the\r\n  task itself is consolidation, minimize TOTAL behavior instead — extract the\r\n  shared stage rather than duplicate a fix (PROCESS.md §3, \"Consolidate, don't\r\n  duplicate — the 0→A→B rule\").\r\n\r\n## Repo map\r\n\r\nEntry points only — read the directories for the rest.\r\n\r\n- **Method:** [`PROCESS.md`](project-trajectory/PROCESS.md) (the load-bearing\r\n  core) + [`PROCESS_OPTIONS.md`](project-trajectory/PROCESS_OPTIONS.md) (opt-in\r\n  layers, each with an *applies-when*). Keep §-numbering stable — `§N`\r\n  cross-refs pervade the kit.\r\n- **The shipped kit:** everything under `project-trajectory/` — the\r\n  `*.template.*` artifact formats and blank `registries/` an adopter fills,\r\n  `scripts/`, `skills/`, `ci/`, and the reference docs `EXAMPLE.md` /\r\n  `ADOPTING.md` / `MULTI_REPO.md`. A `scope: this-repo` skill maintains *this*\r\n  repo (dogfooded into `.claude/skills/`): change a convention here, update its\r\n  skill.\r\n- **The kit's own, filled spine** — not to be confused with those blank forms:\r\n  `docs/requirements/`, `docs/test/`, `docs/work/`, `docs/stack.ini`,\r\n  `docs/runtime-flows.md` (the authored Runtime flows; the structural\r\n  architecture is derived into the dashboard — WI-455), the\r\n  derived `docs/stage` (never hand-set), and the generated root\r\n  [`PROJECT_STATE.html`](PROJECT_STATE.html) (`python\r\n  project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py`). The traced \"product\" is\r\n  `project-trajectory/scripts` + `tests/`; the self-application boundary is **no\r\n  *product* launch** — an actions-menu launcher is in scope, `run.*` product\r\n  launchers are not.\r\n- **Working surfaces:** [`docs/status.md`](docs/status.md), forward-only — what\r\n  happens **next** — and `docs/log.md`, what happened. Root\r\n  `agent-resume.{cmd,sh,command}` run\r\n  `project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py --root .`, resuming from\r\n  `docs/status.md` under the policies declared in\r\n  [`docs/process.toml`](docs/process.toml) — the human-approval hold is the\r\n  `human_approval_through` dial there (do not paraphrase its value here;\r\n  read it), and `push = \"human\"`.\r\n- **Reference, not working surfaces:**\r\n  [`docs/registry-machinery-reference.md`](docs/registry-machinery-reference.md)\r\n  (what the scripts enforce on the spine, field by field),\r\n  [`docs/enforcement-audit.md`](docs/enforcement-audit.md) (each rule mapped to\r\n  its strongest enforcer, gaps included), and\r\n  [`docs/archive/`](docs/archive/README.md) (design history; root stays\r\n  live-only).\r\n- **`tests/`** — the kit's own pytest suite; the stdlib rule covers the kit\r\n  scripts, not testing them. `.github/workflows/test.yml` runs it on Linux +\r\n  Windows (Python 3.11 + latest) and macOS.\r\n\r\n## Communication style\r\n\r\nDirect and concrete; explain the *why* before the *how*; surface trade-offs and\r\nuncertainty honestly; ask before anything irreversible. Prefer the simplest\r\nthing that works and say so when a request looks over-engineered for its need.\r\nDon't change unrelated code; when you spot a design smell, surface it as a\r\nseparate finding rather than fixing it inline. (The shipped guide states the\r\nfull version — `project-trajectory/AGENTS.template.md` \"Working agreement\".)\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content .agents/skills/antidote/SKILL.md; Get-Content .agents/skills/architecture-decomposition/SKILL.md; Get-Content .agents/skills/session-protocol/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content .agents/skills/antidote/SKILL.md; Get-Content .agents/skills/architecture-decomposition/SKILL.md; Get-Content .agents/skills/session-protocol/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\r\nname: antidote\r\ndescription: Use when coding, reviewing, refactoring, auditing, committing fixes, or adding guards, validation, workarounds, or 20+ lines without deleting code — stop and find the root-cause fix instead of patching the symptom.\r\nstacks: [any]\r\ndomains: [any]\r\nphases: [dev]\r\ntags: [root-cause, refactoring, code-review, anti-pattern, vendored]\r\nscope: kit\r\n---\r\n\r\n> **Vendored** from [Avtr99/antidote](https://github.com/Avtr99/antidote)\r\n> `skills/antidote/SKILL.md`, MIT license, source commit\r\n> `8e0350e3d86df36852d56ad0a502376e24de870c` (2026-08-22, upstream version\r\n> 1.1.0; OI-58, WI-507). Content below is verbatim from upstream except this\r\n> note and the frontmatter above, rewritten to this kit's skill schema\r\n> (`skills/README.md`) — the original carried its own `name`/`description` and\r\n> a `license`/`metadata` block this kit's frontmatter contract has no field\r\n> for. This is the same consolidation principle stated at repo scale in\r\n> [`PROCESS.md` §3](../../PROCESS.md) (\"Consolidate, don't duplicate — the\r\n> 0→A→B rule\"): validate and implement once, at the boundary that owns the\r\n> behavior, never re-derived at each caller. The doctrine there governs\r\n> restructuring a codebase; this skill is its per-fix companion at the single\r\n> change in front of you.\r\n\r\n# Antidote\r\n\r\nAI agents often fix symptoms. A bug appears, and the agent adds a check for that one path. The check does not remove the bug. It only hides it on that path. The next path that uses the same data does not have the check. The bug comes back. After many patches, the code holds together with guards that no one remembers.\r\n\r\nBefore you write a fix, ask this: \"What is the smallest change that makes this fix unnecessary?\" Use a stricter type, delete a code path, or use a library. This work looks larger than a patch, but it costs less over time. A patch is cheap today and expensive tomorrow. A structural fix is the reverse.\r\n\r\n## Stop and rethink before you\r\n\r\n- Write a function that only checks, cleans, or coerces a value before the rest of the code uses it. Can a stricter type, schema, or interface make the bad shape impossible to build? Then nothing must check for it.\r\n- Add a defensive guard, such as a null check, an empty catch block, or a \"this should not happen\" branch. Ask why that state is reachable. Fix the cause, not the symptom.\r\n- Work around a library or framework behavior. Read the docs for the intended pattern first. A workaround breaks on the next upgrade. The intended pattern does not.\r\n- Duplicate logic in a new code path. Can both paths use one implementation?\r\n- Write 50 or more lines for a \"simple\" bug. You likely fix a symptom. Find the root cause.\r\n- Add a config flag for an edge case. The edge case may only exist because the design is wrong.\r\n\r\n## A library is one option, not the default answer\r\n\r\nThe goal is the smallest correct fix. Sometimes the fix is a library. Sometimes it is a type. Sometimes it is a deleted code path. Sometimes it is a few honest lines. Do not add a dependency by reflex.\r\n\r\nA library helps when the problem is hard and already solved: retries, caching, pagination, circuit breakers, state machines, config validation, and date parsing. Check what the project already uses before you add a new one.\r\n\r\nA library does not help when the custom code it replaces is only a few lines. Importing a package to avoid one conditional is its own kind of overcomplication. Now you carry its API, its versions, and its bugs. The line to watch is not \"custom code exists.\" It is \"custom code re-implements something hard that a good library already does well.\"\r\n\r\n## Two examples of the same fix\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# PATCH: clear the field on every write path; miss one, and the bug returns\r\ndef sanitize_order(order):\r\n    if order[\"kind\"] == \"digital\":\r\n        order[\"shipping_address\"] = None\r\n    return order\r\n\r\n# FIX: the variant has no such field, so nothing can set it\r\nclass DigitalOrder(BaseModel):\r\n    kind: Literal[\"digital\"]\r\n    download_url: str\r\n\r\nclass PhysicalOrder(BaseModel):\r\n    kind: Literal[\"physical\"]\r\n    shipping_address: Address\r\n```\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# PATCH: every caller defends against the same bad shape\r\ndef process(data):\r\n    if data is None: return\r\n    if \"items\" not in data: return\r\n    for item in data.get(\"items\") or []:\r\n        if not item or \"name\" not in item: continue\r\n        ...\r\n\r\n# FIX: validate once at the boundary, then trust the type\r\nclass RequestData(BaseModel):\r\n    items: list[Item]\r\n\r\ndef process(data: RequestData):\r\n    for item in data.items:\r\n        ...  # item.name is guaranteed\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou can use the same mechanism in any language. TypeScript, Zod, Go structs, Rust enums, Kotlin sealed classes, and Java records let you make an invalid state impossible. You do not need to detect it later.\r\n\r\n## Checklist before you write the fix\r\n\r\n1. State the root cause in one sentence. If you cannot, you do not understand it yet.\r\n2. Check the structure. Would a stricter type, a merged code path, or a deleted feature make this fix unnecessary?\r\n3. Check the library. Use a library only if the problem is hard and already solved. Skip it if a few lines will do.\r\n4. Check what to delete. What code does this fix let you remove? If nothing, you add complexity, not resolve it.\r\n\r\n## Red flags that you are patching, not fixing\r\n\r\n- The fix is 20 or more lines and deletes nothing.\r\n- It is a catch block that swallows an error or silently defaults.\r\n- It guards against a state that \"should not happen.\"\r\n- It re-implements something hard that a good library already does well.\r\n- It works around framework behavior instead of using the framework's pattern.\r\n- It is a new write path instead of reusing the existing one.\r\n\r\n## Audit mode\r\n\r\nWhen the user asks for an audit, do these steps:\r\n\r\n1. Scan for the patterns above. Record the file and line, the pattern, the risk, and the simpler option.\r\n2. Rank by impact. Start with small structural changes that remove large amounts of defensive code.\r\n3. Flag custom retry, cache, validation, or state-machine code. Replace it with a stricter type, structure, or library, whichever is smaller.\r\n4. Output a prioritized plan. Do not start fixing until the user approves it.\r\n\r\n## Fix mode\r\n\r\n1. State the root cause in one sentence.\r\n2. Propose the simplest fix that addresses it. If it is not simpler than what is there, it is the wrong fix.\r\n3. Check if a type change, a structure change, or a library removes the need for custom logic. Pick the smallest option. Do not default to either.\r\n4. Run tests before and after. If there are no tests, write a characterization test first.\r\n5. Delete whatever the fix makes unnecessary.\r\n6. Confirm that the bug is now structurally impossible, not just currently prevented.\r\n\r\n## The test\r\n\r\n> If a new developer adds a code path tomorrow without reading your fix, does the bug come back?\r\n\r\nIf the answer is yes, you wrote a patch. Go back and find the root cause.\r\n---\r\nname: architecture-decomposition\r\ndescription: Use when planning a work item (answering its architectural-delta question) or at DevStg-Arch (breaking down a component scope), and in an architecture audit — name the mode classes, state each boundary's value domain, tabulate output against input dependencies, and apply A8's two descent rules, judged against the shared architecture-decomposition rubric.\r\nstacks: [any]\r\ndomains: [any]\r\nphases: [dev, gate]\r\ntags: [architecture, decomposition, interfaces, components, planning]\r\nscope: kit\r\n---\r\n**When to use.** Three moments: at `DevStg-Arch`, when a component scope is broken into sub-components; when planning a work item, to answer its architectural-delta question; and in an audit of a scope as it stands. *Why:* a boundary is worth its interface only when it hides a decision likely to change, and that is argued from the scope's states, domains and dependencies, not from the steps its processing runs through. The bar is the rubric at `docs/rubrics/architecture-decomposition.md`; this skill is the procedure that produces what the rubric judges, and it cites the rubric's anchors by id instead of restating them.\r\n\r\n**Procedure.**\r\n1. **Name the mode classes** (rubric A2). Record each one, with its transitions, as the scope's state flow in `docs/runtime-flows.md`.\r\n2. **State each boundary's value domain** (A3, A6). Record it as the form of the boundary's interface row in `docs/requirements/interfaces.toml`.\r\n3. **Tabulate output against input dependencies** (A4). Record the table beside the scope's flow in `docs/runtime-flows.md`, or, when planning, in the plan.\r\n4. **Apply A8's two descent rules.** Requirement descent for the scope's obligations; component descent after comparing the candidate cuts, keeping the scope whole among them, under A1, A4 and A7. Record the result in the existing scoped decomposition or review record (process-options.md \"(d2)\"); where the component layer keeps one, that is the component registry's partition record.\r\n\r\nEach output goes only to a home that already exists; the procedure never adds a file or a field to hold it.\r\n\r\n**When planning a work item.** Each step's result answers one clause of the delta question. A new mode class or transition is \"add a state\"; a new case in a domain is \"add a value\"; a new cell in the dependency table is \"a new input\"; a new boundary interface is \"cross a new boundary\". The plan writes its `DELTA:` line from those results, and carries a `within` change as rows of its own.\r\n\r\n**Below a component's boundary**, in its code, use the `deep-module-design` skill.\r\n---\r\nname: session-protocol\r\ndescription: Use when running a work-item (WI) or thread session in this template repo — how to read the plan, execute only the scoped work, pass the gates, write the session/WI log, and commit in this repo's style.\r\nstacks: [any]\r\ndomains: [any]\r\nphases: [dev, gate, release]\r\ntags: [session, improvement-plan, workflow, commit-style, wi]\r\nscope: this-repo\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Session protocol (this template repo)\r\n\r\nHow a WI/thread session runs here. The live homes: **`docs/status.md`** (what's\r\nnext), **`docs/work/`** (the WI registry — one spec file per item, status = its\r\ndirectory), and\r\n**`docs/log.md`** (the session/gate record). This skill is the fast path; the\r\nprocess masters (`PROCESS.md` / `PROCESS_OPTIONS.md`) win when they disagree. The\r\nkit's design history — the old thread specs and the WI-1.x log — is archived at\r\n`docs/archive/IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md` (context, not a working surface).\r\n\r\n## 1. Read before doing\r\n\r\n- Read `CLAUDE.md` (governs editing the kit) and `AGENTS.md` (the pointer stub).\r\n- Read `docs/status.md` (the working surface — what's next) and the scoped WI's\r\n  spec file under `docs/work/` (its directory is its status). The\r\n  spec-of-record for a WI is what its `specref` points at (an SR and/or a plan doc); older landed work is in the\r\n  archived plan. **Do only the scoped work** — no unrelated edits.\r\n- If a stub is being revived, find and link its earlier backlogged form (search\r\n  `docs/archive/scratch.md` + the stub threads) so the resolution is traceable.\r\n- **Check `git status` first.** Residue in the working tree is from an\r\n  interrupted session; reconcile it against the open WI's spec / Done-when\r\n  *before* new work — verify-and-commit what is complete, discard what is not\r\n  part of the scope, and record which in the log. (The unattended loop surfaces\r\n  this into the session prompt; the judgment is yours — it never auto-stashes.)\r\n- **Treat a declared-policy change as a status-staleness event.** In the same\r\n  sitting, grep `docs/status.md` for pause/stop/approval\r\n  language predicated on the old `gate_policy`, `push`, `review_rounds`, or\r\n  `guardrails` value; point to `docs/process.toml` instead of paraphrasing it.\r\n\r\n## 2. Respect the constraints\r\n\r\n- **Byte budgets** on `AGENTS.template.md` (10,000) and `PROCESS.md` — see the\r\n  `byte-budget-guard` skill. Push expansion to `PROCESS_OPTIONS.md` /\r\n  `ADOPTING.md` / `EXAMPLE.md`.\r\n- **Stdlib-only, cross-platform** kit scripts (Python 3.11+, Windows + POSIX).\r\n- **Single source of truth:** state a fact once and link to it; don't paraphrase\r\n  a rule into five files.\r\n\r\n### Standing rules\r\n\r\nRelocated here from `docs/status.md` (WI-477): they are durable doctrine, and\r\nthe working surface is forward-only — what must happen **next**, not what is\r\nalways true.\r\n\r\n- **An id named in `docs/status.md`'s hand-authored prose CANNOT BE CLAIMED.**\r\n  `integrate._status_prose_refusal` refuses it at claim time; generated blocks\r\n  are exempt. Point at `docs/work/queued/` and let the generated frontier name\r\n  ids.\r\n- **Never revert a real fix, or sanction a check, to green a step.** Editing a\r\n  declared list — a coverage floor, an orphan glob, a ratchet baseline — to\r\n  clear a finding IS accepting what it measures. If a ratchet fires on\r\n  legitimate work, re-stamp it deliberately and record the reason.\r\n- **Signed claims + one-machine humility.** The recurring review-era defect was\r\n  signed CLAIMS that pass every test (PROCESS_OPTIONS.md, \"Signed\r\n  measurements\"), and **one machine is one data point** for any OS-behaviour or\r\n  timing claim — state the condition, never the universal.\r\n- **Measure on a tree whose line endings match the index.** Before trusting any\r\n  byte count or hash, run `git ls-files --eol | grep 'w/crlf'` — only\r\n  `*.ps1`/`*.cmd`/`*.bat` should appear.\r\n- **Claiming runs through the integrator** (`integrate.py claim`); merges are\r\n  its serial fail-closed queue, and a pause is a tracked `docs/work/pause`.\r\n- **The coordinator's procedure has one home, the `coordinator-cycle` skill**\r\n  (adjudication, rendered review briefs, the rework coverage gate, disputes).\r\n- **A stopped lane CLOSES; it is never held by renaming its ref (OI-70).** The\r\n  only sanctioned stop is the partial close (§4): the spec moves to the terminal\r\n  `docs/archive/work/partial/` with a handback report an adjudicator then judges\r\n  (queue a successor, or mint an open item). Parking a lane by renaming its\r\n  branch ref away from its `docs/work/active/<branch>/` claim is BANNED — a lane\r\n  must close or it gets lost, as the wi508 hold nearly was — and\r\n  `check_trajectory` reports any active claim with no matching branch ref.\r\n\r\n## 3. End green (gates)\r\n\r\nRun the one declared bar command and paste the real output — never a green\r\nyou didn't produce:\r\n\r\n```\r\nrun.cmd smoke                                          # Windows launcher\r\npython project-trajectory/scripts/run_menu.py smoke    # from a shell\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt must pass before **each** commit — this is the **commit bar**. Its steps\r\nare declared once, in `docs/stack.ini` `[run] smoke`, so a build round's bar\r\ncannot skip the strict lints it carries (review-churn ruling 6, 2026-10-11;\r\nthey never run in the commit hook). The lints run first; the budget check runs\r\nlast, and its run is the bar's only tier run.\r\n`-m smoke` is the fast per-commit tier; `check_smoke_budget.py --mode warn`\r\nruns it once, FAILS the commit on a red tier (with the tier's own exit code),\r\nand reports the tier's CPU-seconds and wall time against `docs/stack.ini`\r\n`[smoke-budget]`, with a WARN line for a limit over. No bar fails on seconds\r\n(the owner, 2026-10-20): `run.cmd audit` judges them, running the same step\r\nwith `--mode enforce` against the tier's own CPU-seconds (`cpu-seconds`;\r\nother load on the box moves it far less than wall time), a looser wall cap (`wall-seconds`)\r\nand the tier's test count (`max-tests`), by hand and at phase close. A breach\r\nthere is filed as a re-tier row, never charged to the lane that ran it. The\r\ntier stays a real smoke test — \"is it basically alive?\", not a re-run of\r\nmost of the suite. Tiering is\r\nopt-out: smoke drops the **subprocess/scaffold-heavy** modules\r\n(`tests/conftest.py` `SLOW_MODULES` — the hook/gate/scaffold/heavy-script runs\r\nthe commit hook and the gate re-exercise anyway), so a **new (in-process) test\r\nis in the bar by default**. Every bar judges the tier's membership through a\r\ndeterministic ratchet against `max-tests`, `tests/test_smoke_budget.py`\r\n(it bites if the tier grows back toward the full suite; re-stamp deliberately,\r\nreason in the log). Run the **full** unfiltered suite (`pytest -q -n auto`)\r\nonce at phase close; mid-phase, only when it demonstrably fits inside one turn,\r\nand never end a turn waiting on one. The\r\nfull `check.py --gate <gate>` is the **gate bar** (unfiltered suite + coverage):\r\nit belongs to gate advancement, phase close, and CI, not to each\r\nmid-phase slice; `--jobs 0` runs its independent steps concurrently. A per-WI\r\nslice inside a phase ends at the commit bar (PROCESS_OPTIONS.md, \"Phase\r\ncadence\"). New behavior needs new tests\r\n(`tests/`); update `test_bootstrap.py` file lists and `README.md` kit-contents /\r\n`bootstrap.py` `MAPPING` when the scaffold surface changes.\r\n\r\n**A session's scratch has one dated root** (WI-840). Every review and\r\nfull-suite run passes `--basetemp`, and every reproduction file goes, under\r\n`review-tmp/<date>-<session>/` (`review-tmp` beside the lane worktrees is the\r\none scratch root the review sandbox may write). Reuse one name per kind of run\r\nacross rounds, never a fresh name per round: pytest empties a literal\r\n`--basetemp` only when that name runs again. Delete the dated root once the\r\nsession's results are recorded.\r\n\r\n## 4. Record the work\r\n\r\n- Close the WI by MOVING its spec file to the terminal directory its outcome\r\n  names, under the archive (WI-504, OI-55 ruled (a)) —\r\n  `docs/archive/work/complete/` when it shipped, `docs/archive/work/cancelled/`\r\n  when it never will, `docs/archive/work/partial/` when you could not finish\r\n  (that one is TERMINAL too, and it owes an immutable per-close report under\r\n  `docs/handbacks/`: the report IS the close event, and the disposition row an\r\n  adjudicator gets keys on its path) — and filling\r\n  its `## Deliverable` body (status is the directory, never a field — and a\r\n  `partial/` close leaves the Deliverable EMPTY, because the report carries the\r\n  record and the spec's definition is deliberately byte-identical), and\r\n  record a session entry for `docs/log.md`: one-line summary, deliverables,\r\n  **deviations from spec**, **byte deltas on budgeted files**, and the\r\n  `pytest -q` totals (match the style already there). A **driven figure** in\r\n  the fragment/Deliverable — a total, a census count, a timing — follows the\r\n  declared-figure convention (process-options.md \"Signed measurements\",\r\n  WI-392): its line carries the producing command + revision (or its\r\n  derivation) under the `fig:` marker, held to presence by\r\n  `check_figures.py`. **Where it goes:** on\r\n  the serial trunk lane, append to `docs/log.md` directly; on a work branch,\r\n  write it as a fragment `docs/log.d/<WI-id>-<slug>.md` (starting with its\r\n  `## <YYYY-MM-DD> — <title>` heading; links authored relative to\r\n  `docs/log.d/`) — `trunk_step.py` compiles fragments into the log in merge\r\n  order and deletes them. Never hand-edit `docs/log.md` on a work branch. A\r\n  session that ends owing the owner a decision **declares it** in the fragment —\r\n  `Deferred open items: OI-45` (or `… none — <why>`), checked at the commit bar\r\n  by `gen_open_items.py --check` (process.md §5, OI-41 ARM 2).\r\n- **A delegated decisions record is the owner's to rule.** Write each entry\r\n  of `docs/decisions/<run>.toml` with `review` empty and no `owner` key. The\r\n  owner sets `owner = \"confirmed\"` or `\"overruled\"`; an overrule's commit\r\n  states the new direction in `review` and files or amends a queued or active\r\n  work item citing `docs/decisions/<run>.toml#D-NNN`, or the ruling-sync step\r\n  and the merge slot refuse it (process-options.md, \"Delegated decisions\r\n  record\").\r\n- **Section order inside the spec file is load-bearing.**\r\n  `check_trajectory.parse_spec_deliverable` clips the body at `## Context`, so\r\n  a `## Deliverable` placed *after* Context parses as EMPTY and the close reds\r\n  (R-A hard error). `## Deliverable` before `## Context`, always.\r\n- **Order the close against the verdict round.** Under `review_rounds >= 1` the\r\n  merge queue wants a verdict that NAMES the branch's current **non-record**\r\n  tree (`docs/reviews/` + `docs/log.d/` + `docs/iteration/` are excluded;\r\n  `docs/work/` is not), so any commit after it that CHANGES that tree buys\r\n  another round — and one that does not, cannot.\r\n  Close **first** — Deliverable filled, spec moved to its terminal folder, any\r\n  approving Status-change commit — and take the final verdict round **last**;\r\n  never hand-merge trunk, since only the station's `refresh` commit is peeled.\r\n  A correction the verdict itself demanded still costs a round: that is the gate\r\n  working, not a defect (process-options.md, \"The LLM-gate verdict protocol\").\r\n  **Do not hand-compile `docs/reviews/WI-<n>-REVIEW-A.md`.** Nothing in the kit\r\n  writes it and the gate no longer asks for it: the round files a logged\r\n  reviewer session produced ARE the record, and the readable per-scope summary\r\n  is generated (`gen_verdict_rollup.py`). The legacy file never clears the\r\n  gate: only a fresh full-lane round from the lane's claim base does, and a\r\n  narrow round's verdict steers iteration only (WI-884).\r\n- Update `docs/status.md` to point at what's next; don't leave a stale \"next\".\r\n- **Coordinator close-out (the context guard, `[coordinator]` in\r\n  `docs/process.toml`).** When
[... byte cap hit — full stream in out/run-logs/ ...]
lent model swap.'}\r\nSN-027 {'status': 'Approved', 'stakeholder_refs': ['STK-02'], 'tags': ['perf', 'unattended', 'scripts'], 'need': 'A team gets more than one piece of ready work moving at once: ready work **fans out across bounded parallel lanes**, while mutation of the integration branch stays **serialized and gated**.', 'why': 'Concurrency is admitted here on structural grounds, not throughput ones: the WI DAG already encodes what may proceed, so a frontier bounded to one lane idles ready work for no reason; and parallel *integration* is precisely how a reviewed process ships an unreviewed tree — so fan-out is admitted only because it narrows back to a single serialized, gated seam before anything lands. No instrument here measures speedup, so a quantified-speedup justification would be unfalsifiable as written; the observability such a claim would need is carried instead by the lane-utilisation report.', 'priority': 'S', 'acceptance': 'A launch with independent ready WIs runs up to the configured worker ceiling (default 2) concurrently in isolated worktrees; every finished branch lands through one serial, fail-closed integrator that runs the declared bar on the composed tree; `--jobs 1` preserves the serial semantic; a declared pause stops claiming and drains what is already in flight; a crash at any lifecycle boundary recovers from Git history alone, without double-assignment or half-integrated authoritative state. Spec of record: `docs/archive/specs/parallel-wi-dispatch.2026-07-20.md` + `docs/concurrency-restructure.md`.'}\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command '$t = git show 26eb622408405b46e51355d59030bc2683d52976:docs/plans/2026-10-11-review-churn/README.md; $a = ($t | Select-String '\"'\"'^## 13'\"\\\\.').LineNumber; \"'$b = ($t | Select-String '\"'\"'^## 14'\"\\\\.').LineNumber; \"'$s = $t[($a-1)..($b-2)]; $s | Select-Object -Skip 100 -First 42; git show 26eb622408405b46e51355d59030bc2683d52976:docs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command '$t = git show 26eb622408405b46e51355d59030bc2683d52976:docs/plans/2026-10-11-review-churn/README.md; $a = ($t | Select-String '\"'\"'^## 13'\"\\\\.').LineNumber; \"'$b = ($t | Select-String '\"'\"'^## 14'\"\\\\.').LineNumber; \"'$s = $t[($a-1)..($b-2)]; $s | Select-Object -Skip 100 -First 42; git show 26eb622408405b46e51355d59030bc2683d52976:docs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md'","aggregated_output":"  - **The owner's position** (revision 17). Make it a kit option in `process.toml`, since autonomy needs in-lane installs when a lane discovers something new. The decider is neither the adjudicator (its scope is the spine and behaviour) nor the builder. It is a quick, independent decision on a carefully built brief, permitted only where the option allows it in lane; otherwise the lane hands back. Something with no method of testing it should not be developed.\r\n  - **The arbiter exists, but it is a narrower role:** `dual-plan-arbiter.template.md` selects one of two anonymized plans. The word is taken (`intake.py`: \"the dual-plan arbiter owns that word\"), so a dependency decider is a new hat on the same pattern: a fresh session, a composed brief, a fixed answer vocabulary.\r\n  - **What the brief must guard against** (integrator).\r\n    - **The package may not be the one intended.** Of the packages that code-generating models import, 5.2% (commercial models) and 21.7% (open-source models) did not exist (Spracklen et al., USENIX Security 2025, 576,000 samples), and attackers register such names (\"slopsquatting\"). A 2026 preprint, not peer reviewed, measures 4.6–6.1% on newer models. So the decider verifies identity (the index entry, the project and repository, the pinned version and hash, the licence) and never takes the builder's name for it.\r\n    - **Lanes share one interpreter.** The coordinator's recipes run every lane on the main checkout's `.venv`. An in-lane `pip install` changes the environment every other lane and trunk use, so one lane's install can turn another's bar green or red. An admitted install needs a lane-local environment, or is applied only from declared files.\r\n    - **An install not in the declared files works on one machine.** Admission is therefore a diff: the ledger row, the dependency file and the lock, reviewed with the lane's change. The install follows from the files, not the reverse.\r\n    - **Tier.** A `shipped` dependency, forced on every adopter, keeps its owner ruling and always hands back. The option covers non-shipped tiers only, such as test tooling, which is also the commonest in-lane case (\"X is needed to test Y\"). An adopter's product runtime dependency is an architecture decision, so it hands back too.\r\n  - **Proposed shape** (integrator, revision 17, for the owner to confirm or overrule).\r\n    - **The setting.** One key in `[policies]`, beside `push`, which it mirrors: the key names the act, and the value names who may do it. `dependency_admission = \"human\"` is the shipped default (superseded by decision 3, revision 19: the value is `\"owner-or-delegate\"`): a lane that needs a new dependency hands back, and the owner rules the ledger row. `\"independent\"` lets a fresh admission session admit one in the lane. An undeclared key reads `\"human\"`, and any other value is refused. The tier limit is fixed by the kit, not a second key: a `shipped` dependency always hands back. `\"independent\"` also requires `[attestation] decision_recording` to be `\"record\"` or stricter, and the loader refuses the pair otherwise, so no admission goes unrecorded.\r\n    - **The hat.** It is a new prompt, `dependency-admission.template.md`, not the arbiter's or the adjudicator's. It runs as a fresh session, independent of the builder and, where available, of another model family. It answers ADMIT, REFUSE (build without it) or HAND-BACK (to the owner).\r\n  - **Who verifies what, in order.**\r\n    1. **The builder stops and asks; it never installs.** The request states the need, which tests need it, what it replaces, and why the standard library or an existing dependency won't do.\r\n    2. **A script gathers the identity facts, and judges none of them.** From the package index: whether the entry exists, its first release date and release history, its project and source-repository links, its licence, the exact version and its hashes, and known advisories (`pip-audit`, already this repo's tooling). It reads the index with `urllib` only. A newly registered, single-release package with no repository is the slopsquatting profile, and the brief shows that plainly.\r\n    3. **The admission session decides** on that brief, before anything is installed in the lane.\r\n    4. **On ADMIT, the change is a diff.** It contains the ledger row, whose \"Ruled\" cell cites the decision entry (`docs/decisions/<run>.toml#D-NNN`), the dependency file, and the regenerated lock. The install comes from the lock, into the lane's own environment, never the shared `.venv`.\r\n    5. **Checks in the lane, before review:** `test_dependency_ledger.py` (the row exists), the lock check (the lock matches the file), and `pip-audit` over the new lock. Today the last two run only in CI on their own schedule (`lock-check.yml` after a test run, `sca.yml` weekly), so a lane whose diff touches the dependency files runs them in its pre-review bar (item 13). An adopter supplies its own stack's equivalents.\r\n    6. **The lane's reviewers** read the dependency diff and the ledger row's argument with the rest of the change.\r\n    7. **The landing bar** runs in an environment built from the lock, so a dependency that works only on one machine fails there.\r\n    8. **The owner reads it afterwards.** The admission is a delegated decision, always listed in the record's `high_risk`, so it heads \"Decisions to review\". An overrule files a WI to remove it, through the existing overrule rule.\r\n  - **When the steps run** (revision 17, the owner's question). Steps 1–4 run where the need is found, before the code that needs it is built. Steps 5–7 are not extra stages: they join the lane's existing order (step 5 in the pre-review bar, after lint and tests; step 6 in the review; step 7 at landing). Step 8 never blocks. There is one path, with two places it can start:\r\n    - **At the scope critique (preferred; corrected in revision 18).** The scope critique names the dependencies a WI needs, so steps 1–4 run before the claim, and a WI whose admission is still pending is not claimable: the scheduler doesn't start work that can't work. The lane is then cut with the admitted lock. The plan itself comes after the claim (decision 4), so a need the in-lane planner finds is handled as in the build case below (Astra).\r\n    - **During the build,** when the builder finds a need the plan missed. The build pauses at step 1 and resumes after step 4. On REFUSE it builds without the dependency; where that is impossible, it hands back.\r\n  - **What \"hands back\" means** (revision 17, the owner's question; the kit's existing mechanism, nothing new).\r\n    - **The lane never stalls.** A partial close is the only sanctioned stop, and a lane is never held (OI-70). The lane closes `partial` (`handback.py`): its work is committed with a keep/discard split, any code the bar refuses is quarantined as a bar-inert `.patch`, and an immutable report is written under `docs/handbacks/`. The branch merges and the run moves on to independent work.\r\n    - **An independent adjudicator judges the close, not the dependency** (`adjudicate-disposition.template.md`). It checks whether the claimed outcome is right and the keep/discard split honest, and it drafts the mandatory successor row.\r\n    - **The dependency question goes to the owner as an open item.** Where what is left is the owner's to rule, the successor carries an `[open_item]` card (one line, blast radius, options, recommendation). The mint makes it a pending OI and writes the OI into the successor's `needs`, so the successor sits queued but blocked until the owner rules.\r\n    - **The card cites the admission facts; it does not redo them.** The owner's scope rule keeps installability out of the adjudicator's judgement. So the handback report carries the builder's request, the gathered identity facts and, under `\"independent\"`, the admission session's reasons, and the card cites them instead of re-judging. The same OI gate serves a need found at planning under `\"human\"`: the queued WI's `needs` names the OI before any claim.\r\n  - **Revision 18: corrections from the revision 17 reviews, and decision 3's options.**\r\n    - **Decision 3, the owner's framing (confirmed in revision 19, below).** The owner expected one value, \"owner or delegate\": the session hands back, the need becomes an open item, and whatever runs the sequence rules it. That may be the owner, or another session the owner has blessed to take delegated decisions. Checked: a lane cannot tell whether it is attended. The only route from a lane to the owner is the partial close and its open item, on the owner's surface (`docs/open-items.html`, PROCESS.md §5). The delegated-decisions record is read afterwards, and the `Loop-Session` trailer marks loop-started commits but tells a lane nothing about who is attending. So \"delegate\" can only act at the ruling of the open item, never inside the lane, which makes the owner's single value the natural one. The options:\r\n      - **(A) Two values (recommended).** `dependency_admission = \"owner-or-delegate\"` is the shipped default: the lane hands back, and the open item is ruled by the owner or by a session acting on the owner's recorded delegation (PROCESS.md §4's shape for a held act: \"the delegation and its scope recorded\"). A delegate's ruling is the owner's act, recorded where the owner's ruling of an open item is recorded (corrected in revision 20; see the authority vocabulary below). `\"independent\"` lets a fresh admission session, never the requester, admit in the lane, attended or not. TOML spelling follows the file's lowercase, hyphenated values (`agent-iteration`, `escalate-first`).\r\n      - **(B) No key: derive it from `human_approval_through`,** as off-spine approvals already are (OI-30 D3), by mapping the ledger to `DevStg-Arch`. In this repo that makes admission independent by default, since that rung isn't held here. It ties supply-chain risk to spine maturity.\r\n      - **(C) A third value for an attended delegate inside the lane.** Dropped, because a lane can't tell, and nothing lets a lane reach a person but the open item.\r\n\r\n      Under every option, a `shipped` dependency is always the owner's. The naming of the shared vocabulary (\"owner\", as PROCESS.md §4 says, against `push`'s \"human\") is part of the confirmation.\r\n\r\n      **Confirmed by the owner (revision 19): option (A),** `dependency_admission = \"owner-or-delegate\" | \"independent\"`, with `\"owner-or-delegate\"` the shipped default, in the owner's own term.\r\n      - **What a delegate is** (owner, revisions 19–20). A delegate can do everything a human owner would do; the human has given an LLM agent explicit permission to act in their stead, and that permission grants whatever the human granted. The kit cannot enforce who is human, and no process can guarantee it: a session running outside the coordinator role and outside `agent-resume`, told it is delegated with full permission, cannot be told apart from the owner. So **owner and delegate are one role, \"owner-or-delegate\"**, and a delegate's act is the owner's act, recorded wherever the owner's would be (an open item's ruling, an approval's commit). It is not a delegated-decisions-record entry.\r\n      - **The distinction the kit can make, and keeps** (integrator, from the owner's answer; revision 20). What the kit *can* identify is an autonomous run: a session the kit's machinery starts, which is `agent-resume`'s loop, the dispatcher, and every worker they spawn. `kitlib/provenance.py` sets the `KIT_LOOP_SESSION` marker in that process (`mark_loop_process`), its children inherit it, and their commits must carry the `Loop-Session` trailer. **Checked: the coordinator role carries no marker**, attended or as a headless leg (`coordinator-tools/overnight_legs.py` sets none). The owner grouped the coordinator role with `agent-resume` as distinguishable; today the kit cannot tell a coordinator session from the owner, so the vocabulary entry below states which side it is on. That is what the existing enforcers already key on: `check.py`'s `held-status` step, the merge slot and the acceptance writer refuse such a session's move of a held status. So no enforcement code changes for delegation (this resolves Astra's revision 19 MAJOR on executable authority): the owner-or-delegate acts unmarked, as the owner does, and an autonomous run stays refused on held acts. The routing table in PROCESS_OPTIONS.md (\"a ruled open item … the exits — never decided under delegation\") is right for autonomous runs, and only its wording conflates them with delegation (Fable's revision 19 MAJOR).\r\n      - **The authority vocabulary, rewritten for consistency** (owner: \"some permissions / designations need to be rewritten … including some settings changing labelling\"; proposed, with item 10's TOML migration, as one RESYNC entry since adopters rename values):\r\n        - **PROCESS.md §4:** \"A held rung's approval is the owner's act, taken by the owner-or-delegate (anyone the owner has given that permission, human or agent; the kit cannot tell them apart). An autonomous run (a session the machinery starts, marked by its `Loop-Session` trailer) never approves on a held rung.\" The CLARITY re-attest case by an independent adjudicator stays as stated.\r\n        - **PROCESS_OPTIONS.md:** \"Delegated decisions record\" becomes the **autonomous decisions record**: the calls an autonomous run makes, recorded for the owner-or-delegate to confirm or overrule. Its routing table's \"never decided under delegation\" becomes \"never decided by an autonomous run\".\r\n        - **`process.toml` labels:** `push = \"human\"` becomes `\"owner-or-delegate\"`; the `decision_recording` comments' \"a DELEGATED run\" become \"an autonomous run\"; `human_approval_through`'s \"a human approves\" becomes \"the owner-or-delegate approves\", and whether the key itself is renamed (`owner_approval_through`) is part of the same entry. (Ruled in revision 35: the term is \"human-or-delegate\", and the key is renamed `human_or_delegate_approval_through`.)\r\n# Rubric — Plan decomposition (WI-190)\n\n**Adjudicates:** the dual-plan cross-critique and arbiter rounds\n(process-options master, \"Dual-plan decomposition\") — whether a proposed WI\ndecomposition is worth building, judged as **artifacts against the goal brief\nand the coverage report**, never as a conversation.\n**Used by:** the plan-critic and arbiter hats\n(`project-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-*.template.md`). `plan_coverage.py`\nalready computes clause coverage and resolves refs; this rubric judges what\ncode cannot — are the WIs solvable, is the coverage honest, is the\ndecomposition non-redundant?\n\n**Second use: a single plan** (WI-913). The same anchors judge the in-lane\nplan of one claimed work item whose claim copy declares `planmode =\n\"single\"` (`review_brief.py critique --plan`). There, a plan's `Plan-WI` rows\nare that one work item's build steps, not successor WIs; the goal brief is\nthe item's Done-when (its `D#` clauses); and there is one critic, no rival\nplan and no arbiter. Judge each row as a step one build session completes,\nthe coverage report (for a first plan, computed over the Done-when alone;\nfor a revised plan, over the Done-when and the first critique's finding ids,\nnever their text), and the plan's\n`DELTA:` line: does the work item add a state, add a value to a domain, make\nan output depend on a new input, or cross a new boundary, and if so does the\nplan carry the change within its scope?\n\nEvery verdict **cites anchor ids**. The critic's verdict line is\n`VERDICT: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED findings=N`; the arbiter's is\n`VERDICT: SELECT <plan> ports=N`, each port a cited delta\n(\"port B-3 — closes C4, anchored DevStg-Tests\").\n\n**Transfer caveat (state wherever this rubric is applied):** the\ndebate/selection evidence behind this protocol comes from QA, math, and code\nwith objective verifiers — nothing benchmarks it on *plan artifacts*, and\ntwo-planner reconciliation evaluated for plan quality is an open research gap.\nThis rubric is the best-supported extrapolation, not a proven instrument\n([co-planning knowledge pack](../knowledge/co-planning.md), retrieved\n2026-07-16).\n\n## Anchors\n\n**DevStg-Reqs — Solvable unit.** Each proposed WI is completable by one build session at\nits tier: a first-time implementer could name the deliverable and a checkable\ndone-condition from the row alone. *Bad:* \"improve the dispatch layer\" (no\ndeliverable); a row whose scope needs three sessions and two design rulings.\n\n**DevStg-Tests — Complete coverage.** Every goal clause is covered by some WI **or its\nexclusion is declared** in the plan's notes as a non-goal with a reason —\nnever silence. The coverage report computes the gap; the critic judges whether\na declared exclusion is honest.\n\n**DevStg-Impl — Non-redundant decomposition.** No two WIs cover the same clause without\na declared split reason; scope boundaries are crisp enough that two\nimplementers would not collide. Multi-covered clauses in the coverage report\nare the tripwire; the judgment is whether the overlap is a real seam or a\nduplicated scope.\n\n**G4 — Coherent DAG.** Every predecessor edge reflects a real artifact-level\ndependency (an edge the planner could defend by naming what the successor\nconsumes), and no real dependency is missing. `plan_coverage.py` catches\nunknown ids and cycles; the phantom edge and the missing edge are judgment\ncalls.\n\n**G5 — The delta answer holds against the shared rubric.** A single plan's\n`DELTA:` line is judged against\n[architecture-decomposition](architecture-decomposition.md). Each of these is\na G5 finding, citing the rubric anchor it rests on:\n- a `none` that a row contradicts, because the row adds a mode class (A2), a\n  domain case (A3), a dependency cell (A4) or a boundary interface (A6);\n- a `within` whose rows do not carry the interface and domain edits;\n- a `beyond` that plans around the refactor.\n\nThe planner's procedure for the answer is the `architecture-decomposition`\nskill.\n\n**B1 — Seam duplication** *(imports\n[spec-interface-hygiene](spec-interface-hygiene.md) B1).* A row's `Proposed:`\nseam near-duplicates an existing `IF-###` instead of consuming or amending it,\nor its rationale does not truly name the nearest existing seam. Judge as a\nfirst-time reader of `interfaces.toml`, not as the plan's author.\n\n**B2 — Coverage laundering.** A row cites clauses its title and deliverable do\nnot actually deliver — citation as decoration to win the coverage diff. The\npre-pass counts citations; only a reader catches a hollow one.\n\n**B3 — Padding.** A WI that exists to look thorough: its deliverable restates\nanother row's done-condition, or a split adds coordination cost without an\nindependently testable deliverable. **More WIs is not better** — a 4-row plan\ndoes not beat a 3-row plan for count or prose volume.\n\n**B4 — Phantom or missing dependency.** A predecessor edge with no\nartifact-level dependency behind it (sequencing habit), or an absent edge\nwhere one WI plainly consumes another's deliverable — either way the DAG lies\nabout buildable order.\n\n## Verdict\n\nOne `- [BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR] <anchor> -> where/why -> the concrete change ->\n@owner` line per finding, each citing an anchor id, then the brief's one\nmachine line. Severity is tied to reachability (review-churn item 4): a\nBLOCKER or MAJOR states a defect in the plan that would reasonably cost a build\nor review round in normal use, and a fail-open, data-loss or security path\ncounts only when a supported workflow or the kit's own routes reach it; a rare\nor contrived path is MINOR at most, or dismissed under PROCESS.md §6 \"Review\nthreat model\". The second critique of a single plan takes the stricter rule\nits own brief states.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
